-0.9537251591682434,-0.33565011620521545,-0.003126021707430482,-2.2291887944447808e-05,-6.794906312279636e-06,-4.768370445162873e-07,-0.19506743550300598,-0.004439498297870159,-0.08748991787433624,-0.3801569938659668,-0.3893837630748749,0.0,-3.8980677345534787e-05,-0.6230388879776001,-0.30393949151039124,-0.17406214773654938,-0.9408963918685913,-0.00014351768186315894,-1.311301275563892e-06,-0.045871708542108536,-0.0005707303644157946],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,400,64,4779,400,65,4779,400,66,4779,400,67,4779,323,400,68,3,387,279,12460,19703,315,279,23606,400,87,61,20,488,220,22,87,61,19,481,220,17,284,220,15,2418,220,7379,198,59,7265,90,6612,9,532,5,59,37018,90,64,61,19,15170,7,64,481,293,2376,64,481,272,2376,64,481,294,2376,64,481,384,9139,488,1124,37018,90,65,61,19,15170,7,65,481,264,2376,65,481,272,2376,65,481,294,2376,65,481,384,9139,90155,5,59,31610,488,1124,37018,90,66,61,19,15170,7,66,481,264,2376,66,481,293,2376,66,481,294,2376,66,481,384,9139,488,1124,37018,90,67,61,19,15170,7,67,481,264,2376,67,481,293,2376,67,481,272,2376,67,481,384,9139,90155,5,59,31610,488,1124,37018,90,68,61,19,15170,7,68,481,264,2376,68,481,293,2376,68,481,272,2376,68,481,294,9139,624,59,408,90,6612,9,92,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ $d,$ and $e$ be the distinct roots of the equation $x^5 + 7x^4 - 2 = 0.$  Find\n\\begin{align*}\n&\\frac{a^4}{(a - b)(a - c)(a - d)(a - e)} + \\frac{b^4}{(b - a)(b - c)(b - d)(b - e)} \\\\\n&\\quad + \\frac{c^4}{(c - a)(c - b)(c - d)(c - e)} + \\frac{d^4}{(d - a)(d - b)(d - c)(d - e)} \\\\\n&\\quad + \\frac{e^4}{(e - a)(e - b)(e - c)(e - d)}.\n\\end{align*}","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"175283ef9db316cebac0e7ac39fac6f9f931df72facfd7e88e41c3ee8220735e"},"seconds":69.12108898162842,"seed":642247981587605449,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the given problem, we need to evaluate the expression:\n\\[\nS = \\frac{a^4}{(a - b)(a - c)(a - d)(a - e)} + \\frac{b^4}{(b - a)(b - c)(b - d)(b - e)} + \\frac{c^4}{(c - a)(c - b)(c - d)(c - e)} + \\frac{d^4}{(d - a)(d - b)(d - c)(d - e)} + \\frac{e^4}{(e - a)(e - b)(e - c)(e - d)}\n\\]\nwhere \\(a, b, c, d, e\\) are the roots of the polynomial \\(P(x) = x^5 + 7x^4 - 2\\).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the expression in a more general form. Consider the polynomial \\(P(x) = x^5 + 7x^4 - 2\\). The roots \\(a, b, c, d, e\\) satisfy the equation:\n\\[\nP(a) = a^5 + 7a^4 - 2 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\nP(b) = b^5 + 7b^4 - 2 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\nP(c) = c^5 + 7c^4 - 2 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\nP(d) = d^5 + 7d^4 - 2 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\nP(e) = e^5 + 7e^4 - 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe need to evaluate:\n\\[\nS = \\sum_{\\text{cyc}} \\frac{a^4}{(a - b)(a - c)(a - d)(a - e)}\n\\]\n\nTo proceed, we use the concept of Lagrange interpolation. The Lagrange interpolation formula for a polynomial \\(Q(x)\\) of degree 4 that passes through the points \\((a, a^4), (b, b^4), (c, c^4), (d, d^4), (e, e^4)\\) is given by:\n\\[\nQ(x) = \\sum_{i} a_i^4 \\prod_{j \\neq i} \\frac{x - a_j}{a_i - a_j}\n\\]\nwhere \\(a_i \\in \\{a, b, c, d, e\\}\\).\n\nSince \\(Q(x)\\) is a polynomial of degree 4, it can be written as:\n\\[\nQ(x) = \\sum_{\\text{cyc}} \\frac{a^4}{(a - b)(a - c)(a - d)(a - e)} \\prod_{j \\neq a} (x - a_j)\n\\]\n\nWe know that the polynomial \\(P(x) = x^5 + 7x^4 - 2\\) can be expressed in terms of its roots:\n\\[\nP(x) = (x - a)(x - b)(x - c)(x - d)(x - e)\n\\]\n\nThus, we can write:\n\\[\nQ(x) = \\sum_{\\text{cyc}} \\frac{a^4}{(a - b)(a - c)(a - d)(a - e)} \\frac{P(x)}{x - a}\n\\]\n\nEvaluating \\(Q(x)\\) at \\(x = 0\\):\n\\[\nQ(0) = \\sum_{\\text{cyc}} \\frac{a^4}{(a - b)(a - c)(a - d)(a - e)} \\frac{P(0)}{-a}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P(0) = -2\\), we have:\n\\[\nQ(0) = \\sum_{\\text{cyc}} \\frac{a^4}{(a - b)(a - c)(a - d)(a - e)} \\frac{-2}{-a} = 2 \\sum_{\\text{cyc}} \\frac{a^3}{(a - b)(a - c)(a - d)(a - e)}\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\(Q(x)\\) is the polynomial that interpolates \\(x^4\\) at the roots \\(a, b, c, d, e\\), we know that \\(Q(x) = x^4\\). Therefore, \\(Q(0) = 0^4 = 0\\).\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n2 \\sum_{\\text{cyc}} \\frac{a^3}{(a - b)(a - c)(a - d)(a - e)} = 0 \\implies \\sum_{\\text{cyc}} \\frac{a^3}{(a - b)(a - c)(a - d)(a - e)} = 0\n\\]\n\nSince the sum of the terms involving \\(a^3\\) is zero, and noting that the original expression involves \\(a^4\\), we conclude:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{7}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
